# mutable_array.bot -- the "mutable_array" namespace: the ordinary Botlish
# members of the MutableArray family (core/mutarray.tcl; MUTABLE-ARRAY.md).
#
# The namespace's intrinsics are root natives registered under their
# qualified names -- mutable_array::allocate, ::create, ::from_list,
# ::generate, ::capacity, ::at, ::set, ::swap, ::copy, ::freeze
# (core/mutarray.tcl) -- and this module may never define a member of the
# same name (surface/modules.tcl, DUPLICATE-NATIVE). The one member below is
# an ordinary function over those intrinsics (STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md):
#
#   mutable_array::get(array, i, d)     the element at i, or D when i does
#                                       not designate an element: exactly
#                                       mutable_array::at(array, i) with its
#                                       IndexNotFound -- and nothing else --
#                                       handled by returning D.
#
# The constructors are intrinsics, each with a static ownership role for
# what it does with the value it is given (core/native.tcl's -ownership):
#
#   mutable_array::create(n, x)      x is *repeated*: one value placed in n
#                                    slots, so x must be unrestricted (an
#                                    affine x is AFFINE-DUPLICATION-
#                                    UNSUPPORTED)
#   mutable_array::from_list(xs)     xs *moves* into the array, which owns
#                                    every element it had
#   mutable_array::generate(n, f)    f is a *factory*: called once per slot,
#                                    in index order, each result owned by
#                                    its slot (affine results included)
#
# They used to be ordinary functions here, typed at their calls by a
# precomputed container rule (hir/containers.tcl) that hid their bodies from
# the ownership discipline: `create(2, make_coroutine())` compiled and stored
# one coroutine in two slots. An intrinsic's roles are what the discipline
# reads, so no library body can do what the same body written in a program
# could not (MUTABLE-ARRAY.md, "Library ownership").
#
# get's DEFAULT is an ordinary argument, evaluated before the call like any
# other (no lazy default), and it is not an optional/null result: a present
# index gives the element, IndexNotFound gives DEFAULT, and every other
# failure of mutable_array::at (a non-MutableArray or non-Int argument)
# propagates unchanged. get copies the element out, so an array of affine
# elements cannot use it (its at is AFFINE-ELEMENT-COPY-OUT): swap the
# element out instead.

fn get(array, index, default):
    value = mutable_array::at(array, index):
        on IndexNotFound:
            default
    value
